gtk: vmlab: every key goes to the guest while the window has the keyboard

While the Windows window has the keyboard, the desktop's own shortcuts (the Windows key,
Alt+Tab) go to the guest too: the window asks the compositor for a shortcuts inhibitor
(keyboard-shortcuts-inhibit-unstable-v1) for its surface, once, when it first has the
keyboard, and keeps it until it is unmapped. The compositor applies it only while the
window has the keyboard, so another window has the desktop's shortcuts as before. The
pointer is not held.

Not GDK's keyboard grab at each change of focus: GNOME asks the person once whether to
allow it, its question takes the keyboard for a moment, and dropping the request then
withdraws the question, so it is never answered.

When the compositor takes the inhibitor back ("inactive": GNOME's Super+Escape), it is
asked for anew the next time the window gains the keyboard; GNOME answers that as the
person answered before, without asking.

Only with VMLAB_WINDOW, on Wayland.
---
